WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.367 --> 00:00:03.870
Are you looking for a secure and efficient way
to manage your containers?

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:08.341
Or maybe you've already heard of PodMan as a Docker alternative,
and you're looking to learn more.

00:00:08.441 --> 00:00:10.410
Well, your search ends here.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:14.514
Today, we're going to be doing a deep dive
into what PodMan is as a container engine.

00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:17.851
Now, my name is Cedric Clyburn. I'm a developer advocate at Red Hat.

00:00:18.151 --> 00:00:23.523
And the IBM folks have graciously allowed me to come in today
to introduce PodMan and show you not only what PodMan is,

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:26.059
but how it works and how to get started.

00:00:26.059 --> 00:00:31.564
So really quickly, before we dive into what PodMan is,
let's quickly talk about why containers are so crucial

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:33.566
and set up the scene for PodMan.

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:37.837
Now let's say we're a developer
and we just finished developing our application.

00:00:37.871 --> 00:00:43.376
Let's say maybe it's a Java app or could be Node or it could be Python,
whatever it may be.

00:00:43.743 --> 00:00:48.048
Now say we want to run it on another developer's computer
or maybe run it in the cloud.

00:00:48.148 --> 00:00:53.253
How do we take this application along with its code base, its runtime,

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:57.123
maybe it's using the JDK and its libraries

00:00:57.891 --> 00:01:01.461
and ensure that this is compatible on maybe another developer's machine

00:01:01.795 --> 00:01:05.298
or different environments such as QA, development and prod.

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:10.737
Well, this is where containerization comes
in effectively encapsulating your entire application

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:16.176
into one single transferable unit
that can run the same way anywhere you want to deploy it.

00:01:16.176 --> 00:01:19.179
So say it's our colleague's computer.

00:01:19.546 --> 00:01:23.216
We can run it over there, or we can take our same application

00:01:23.583 --> 00:01:26.686
and run it anywhere on a server in the hybrid cloud.

00:01:27.187 --> 00:01:30.423
This effectively gets rid of the “oh, it works on my machine” issue

00:01:30.423 --> 00:01:33.460
that we has developers face way too often.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.129
Now this is where PodMan comes in.

00:01:36.129 --> 00:01:38.131
So PodMan is a container engine.

00:01:38.131 --> 00:01:42.302
It's an open source project that helps us with developing containers.

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:49.709
It helps us with managing both the containers and the images,
and it helps us to deploy containers.

00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:55.815
So it's a super powerful tool and it has two unique features about it
that make it different from other container engines.

00:01:56.182 --> 00:01:58.351
Firstly, is its daemon-less approach.

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:02.255
Now if we look at another popular container engine such as Docker,

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:05.992
what will notice is that when we're working with the Docker CLI

00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:10.663
on our terminal or maybe using Docker desktop, what's actually happening

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:15.068
is we're doing functions
such as building an image or running a container.

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:19.806
And what we're actually doing
is talking to something in the background called the Docker Daemon.

00:02:19.806 --> 00:02:21.007
Now what is the Docker daemon?

00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:25.678
Well, it's commonly referred to as the Docker engine,
and it's responsible for all the heavy lifting.

00:02:25.712 --> 00:02:32.418
So it'll create containers for us, it'll help us build images,
and it will do a lot of other functions,

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:36.623
but it's running in the background as a system process
and it's running as root.

00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:39.526
So we have this daemon that's responsible for all the heavy lifting.

00:02:39.526 --> 00:02:42.061
This is what's known as a client/server architecture.

00:02:42.495 --> 00:02:45.398
Now PodMan is a little bit different in its architecture.

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:48.735
PodMan uses something known as the fork exec model.

00:02:48.735 --> 00:02:54.474
So instead of having this background daemon process
running in the background to create containers, work with images.

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:57.076
Now we're doing everything from the client itself.

00:02:57.076 --> 00:03:04.117
So when we're creating a container, PodMan is actually forking itself
and this child process then becomes the container.

00:03:04.551 --> 00:03:07.887
So this difference in architecture makes PodMan unique

00:03:07.887 --> 00:03:12.392
and makes it more efficient, lightweight and secure
when it comes to container engines.

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:16.629
Now, this also brings us to the point of something
called a single point of failure.

00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:23.403
Say, for example, that our Docker engine running in the background
would suddenly crash, become unresponsive or be quit.

00:03:23.636 --> 00:03:29.275
Now we no longer have access to the containers, images and anything else
that's attached to the Docker engine.

00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:32.612
So this is what makes PodMan unique is its daemon-less approach.

00:03:32.946 --> 00:03:37.383
But because of this architecture,
this brings us into the root versus rootless idea.

00:03:37.917 --> 00:03:42.055
So say we're having an application running on our computer.

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:44.390
So say we're running it as root.

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:47.260
Of course, that's going to bring in several security implications.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.731
Say there's a vulnerability,
like we commonly see with a lot of applications nowadays.

00:03:52.098 --> 00:03:55.802
If that was to be exploited and taken advantage of by a hacker,

00:03:56.135 --> 00:04:00.273
now the application could go rogue and our entire system is at risk.

00:04:00.607 --> 00:04:05.478
But say we had the same application and we're running it
not as root, just as a regular user.

00:04:05.945 --> 00:04:08.314
Well, we have the same vulnerability.

00:04:08.314 --> 00:04:11.251
However, if a hacker were to exploit this vulnerability,

00:04:11.551 --> 00:04:15.154
the container, the application is only running as a non root user,

00:04:15.555 --> 00:04:19.959
so only our user could be affected.

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:24.364
This effectively adds another level of security as a container engine

00:04:24.631 --> 00:04:27.634
and is the difference between the approaches of Docker,

00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:33.406
where the Docker engine is running in the background
as root and PodMan, with its root-less approach

00:04:33.973 --> 00:04:39.512
and daemon-less approach, allows you to run by default containers
just as a user and not as root.

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:44.083
Now you'll notice some similarities
when we drew the architecture of Docker and PodMan.

00:04:44.384 --> 00:04:48.121
This is because they're both based on something called the OCI,

00:04:48.254 --> 00:04:52.892
which is the Open Container Initiative,
which has some standards for how we work with container technologies.

00:04:53.259 --> 00:05:00.300
This means that if you're coming from Docker and you're using PodMan, a
lot of the commands such as pulling, pushing and building are the same.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:05.772
So let's go down here and actually check this out with
some of the PodMan commands that we would use on a day-to-day basis.

00:05:06.172 --> 00:05:08.641
So say we want to pull the container down from a registry.

00:05:09.042 --> 00:05:12.111
So say we have the Nginx container right here.

00:05:12.111 --> 00:05:15.817
We would do “podman pull"

00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:18.551
and then give the name of the image that we want to pull down.

00:05:18.551 --> 00:05:20.853
So for this one, we can do Nginx.

00:05:21.321 --> 00:05:27.093
Now we're pulling from our default Docker hub or k8s.io registry.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:28.928
We're pulling down this image.

00:05:28.928 --> 00:05:34.866
Now that we've got this image in our local registry.,
now we can run it with “podman run".

00:05:34.867 --> 00:05:40.473
And of course we can add any flags here that we'd like to, to expose
ports, to mount volumes, whatever it might be.

00:05:40.473 --> 00:05:45.812
So we'll also call the Nginx image right there.

00:05:45.912 --> 00:05:48.781
Now that we have a running container on our system.

00:05:48.915 --> 00:05:56.149
To list it, and then the other active containers, we can do “podman ps” and this will list any active containers.

00:05:56.522 --> 00:06:00.393
Now this is just a small subset of the PodMan commands
that we use on a day-to-day basis.

00:06:00.393 --> 00:06:06.466
And you'll notice it's very similar to Docker if you have Docker
experience, but PodMan doesn't just stop at the commands.

00:06:06.466 --> 00:06:10.203
PodMan goes a step further with something known as PodMan pods.

00:06:10.203 --> 00:06:14.006
So much like Kubernetes pods, a pod is a group of one

00:06:14.006 --> 00:06:17.910
or more containers that share the same volume and the same network.

00:06:18.211 --> 00:06:25.318
So think of it as a single host network where both of the containers
can talk to each other with seamless networking.

00:06:25.485 --> 00:06:28.788
Now let's say we have our job application here
that we've been working on.

00:06:29.222 --> 00:06:34.627
This could be a back end for our application
and we can use this Nginx image that we pull down earlier

00:06:35.061 --> 00:06:39.365
as a Nginx container working as a reverse proxy to route the traffic.

00:06:39.365 --> 00:06:44.370
Now with PodMan, we can run this pod and additionally
we can export the manifest to our cluster.

00:06:44.771 --> 00:06:49.108
So if we're learning Kubernetes and we're using, say, minikube,

00:06:49.108 --> 00:06:53.246
we can export this directly to our cluster.

00:06:53.246 --> 00:06:56.115
So PodMan can be super helpful with learning Kubernetes.

00:06:56.582 --> 00:06:59.986
So to recap everything -- in this fast changing world

00:06:59.986 --> 00:07:05.425
of containerization and microservices,
PodMan is a robust container engine that helps with security,

00:07:05.892 --> 00:07:10.096
with efficiency,
and with seamless deployment of your containerized applications.

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:13.199
So thanks so much for watching. My name is Cedric Clyburn.

00:07:13.466 --> 00:07:17.303
Stay tuned to the channel for more lightboard videos
and have a great day.

00:07:18.204 --> 00:07:20.006
Thanks for watching this video as always.

00:07:20.006 --> 00:07:24.444
Don't forget to like and subscribe and we'll see you in the next one.

